"I am writing to apply for the Data Scientist position in the Technology Solutions squad at Twenty First Group. I am currently completing an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London, after graduating from Imperial College London. Across my academic and professional experience, I have built probabilistic and machine learning models in Python and SQL, worked with large and often messy datasets, and developed tools that turn model outputs into practical insights. I am particularly interested in this role because it combines probabilistic modelling, data engineering, and direct work with clients around live sporting events.",

"What attracts me most to Twenty First Group is the opportunity to develop the models behind custom-built solutions for sports events and properties, delivered through broadcast, digital and fan-facing products. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved. I follow sport closely, particularly swimming, volleyball and tennis. Golf is less familiar to me, and I would want to learn shot-level data and measures such as strokes gained properly rather than assume I already understand them.",

"In my current role at TikTok Shop UK, I query, clean and explore large merchant and transaction datasets in SQL and Python to identify what is actually driving marketplace performance. I build and maintain the pipelines and feature logic behind recurring analysis rather than relying on someone else to provide them, and I build AI agents that automate recurring analytical and reporting workflows, using Claude Code as part of my day-to-day development. I also define the KPI frameworks and dashboards that operations teams use to set targets, and present findings to product and commercial stakeholders.",

"In my previous role at DS Digital Technology, I worked on a patient-facing medical agent for a pharmaceutical client. I co-built the knowledge base behind it, a medically reviewed knowledge graph of 123 entities and 161 relations used to ground the agent's answers, and designed blinded paired A/B tests to measure whether grounding actually improved answer quality rather than assuming that it would. I built the Python pipeline underneath it, connecting six model APIs with retrieval augmentation and implementing concurrency, retries, structured validation and checkpointing so that long runs completed reliably. It generated and scored more than 2,400 responses against a 246-question benchmark and six-dimension rubric that the client adopted as their acceptance standard. I also mined 4,800 patient consultation threads using a regex and LLM extraction pipeline to turn unstructured text into usable data.",

"My MSc dissertation has given me direct experience with probabilistic modelling across the full model lifecycle. Using longitudinal biobank data, I engineered features across multiple time points and compared logistic regression against three tree ensembles to predict cardiovascular events in 458,840 participants. Discrimination was modest, with AUC between 0.714 and 0.718, but calibration was close to exact, which is what makes predicted probabilities safe to act on. I then used doubly robust causal forests on 71,428 participants to estimate individual risk under different lifestyle interventions, tested the sensitivity of those results to unmeasured confounding, and deployed an interactive Streamlit simulation that lets a user vary the inputs and see the modelled effect. Several results were null, and I reported them as null.",

"My earlier experience as a Strategy Analyst Intern at Ipsos Strategy3 prepared me to work with stakeholders across technical and commercial settings. I structured evidence from more than 80 expert interviews into comparable variables, built market-sizing and forecasting models identifying approximately \\$20M in revenue potential, and worked with research and strategy colleagues to turn analytical findings into executive dashboards and client recommendations. This strengthened my ability to explain assumptions clearly and refine my work in response to feedback, which I expect to matter when delivering against fixed event deadlines.",

"I would bring Twenty First Group solid Python and SQL, experience building and validating probabilistic models end to end, and a genuine interest in owning the pipeline that feeds a model rather than handing that problem to someone else. I hold the right to work in the UK without sponsorship until January 2029 and I am based in London.",

"Thank you for considering my application. I would welcome the opportunity to discuss how my experience aligns with the Data Scientist role and the work of the Technology Solutions squad.",
